---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { emph } from "../lib/text.js";
import Distribution from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Distribution.astro";
const rows = [
  ["Spain", "888", "1:52,649", "4,882"],
  ["Uruguay", "431", "1:7,962", "896"],
  ["Argentina", "293", "1:145,882", "12,355"],
  ["Mexico", "223", "1:556,620", "9,555"],
  ["United States", "107", "1:3,387,467", "192,132"],
  ["South Africa", "61", "1:888,159", "57,770"],
  ["Haiti", "49", "1:218,039", "8,036"],
  ["Chile", "14", "1:1,258,320", "22,898"],
  ["France", "11", "1:6,038,429", "280,838"],
];
/* DRAWN BY DENSITY, NOT BY TOTAL, because that is what this page argues. A
   chart ranked by bearers puts Spain at the top and picks it out in the accent,
   and the section directly below it says in so many words that Spain is not the
   answer - Uruguay carries the name at six times the homeland's density, which
   is the signature of founder emigration. A chart that contradicted its own
   caption would be worse than no chart. */
const rate = (i) => 1 / Number(String(i).split(":")[1].replace(/,/g, ""));
const dist = rows
  .map(([c, b, i, r]) => [c, rate(i), `${b} \u00b7 ${i.replace(":", " in ")} \u00b7 rank ${r} in country`])
  .sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
import dItalyLerenaLerina from "../data/italy-lerena-lerina.json";
import BranchRivers from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/BranchRivers.astro";
import { ledger } from "@daviddef/archive-kit/lib/lines.js";
import dVariants from "../data/name-variants.json";
import dSweep from "../data/variant-parent-sweep.json";
import dNavarrete from "../data/navarrete-logrono.json";
import drawing from "../data/lerena-lines.json";
import directLine from "../data/direct-line.json";
import dPeople from "../data/people.json";
/* THE DRAWING IS THE KIT'S AND ITS NUMBERS COME FROM ITS DATA. tools/build-lines.py makes lerena-lines.json from
   data/lerena-lines.tsv, data/lerena-lines-unplaced.tsv and data/people-lines.tsv; ledger() counts it. A number of lines
   typed in this page would be wrong the day a lane is added, so none is. */
const LED = ledger(drawing);
const WORD = ["no", "one", "two", "three", "four", "five", "six", "seven", "eight", "nine", "ten", "eleven", "twelve"];
const nLines = WORD[LED.lanes] ?? String(LED.lanes);
const NLines = nLines[0].toUpperCase() + nLines.slice(1);
const lineNames = Object.fromEntries(dPeople.map((p) => [p.slug, p.name.replace(/\s*\([^)]*\)/g, "").replace(/\s+/g, " ").trim()]));
---
<Base title="The Name"
      eyebrow="2,124 in the world"
      dek="Lerena is a rare surname — rare enough that rarity itself becomes a research tool. It may also be two separate names sharing one spelling. Both facts shape everything else in this archive.">
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-lines">{NLines} lines of one name, drawn side by side</h2>
  <p class="dek">They may not be one family, and this archive has never been able to say that they
    are. {NLines} lines, each with the years it can be documented over and a word for how far back its
    evidence reaches &mdash; and, under the rule, everything the name holds that sits on none of them.</p>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>A lane here has to have both ends.</strong> A beginning that is a document and a
      destination that is a document. Where this archive knows a household is real but cannot say
      which line it belongs to, it goes <em>below</em> the rule rather than onto the nearest lane
      &mdash; because a picture that places everything is a picture that is lying about something.
      What lies beyond a lane&rsquo;s beginning is said under its title: <em>origin inferred</em> where
      the archive has a reason and no document, <em>origin not found</em> where it has neither.</p>
    <p><strong>The red line is drawn broken on purpose.</strong> It is the one the archive exists
      for, and its left end is open: Pablo Armando Lerena&rsquo;s parents are named on an army card
      in 1908 and <strong>appear in no other record anywhere</strong>. Every lane above it is a
      candidate and <strong>not one has been shown to hold them</strong>.</p>
  </div>
  <BranchRivers rivers={drawing} title={drawing.title} names={lineNames} directLine={directLine}
         caption={`Hover or tab any dot for what the document behind it actually says; a dot that links to a person opens that person. The lanes are coloured only to tell them apart &mdash; no colour means kinship, and no two lanes here have been joined to each other. <strong>Of the ${nLines}, two begin in a parish register and one begins with a man holding office; the other two begin before any document of their own &mdash; a birth at Rosario whose baptism has never been found, and a birth in Spain in the 1860s known only from later records.</strong> The number under a line is the people this archive has placed on it through the parent and child links it holds, with a birth year before the living line or a death on their page; it is not everyone who descended, and nobody living is listed.`} />
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><strong>Where each household below the rule is worked.</strong> The reason each one is drawn there is the hover text in the figure; the pages that hold the records are these.
    <ul style="margin:8px 0 0">
      <li>Juan Carlos Lerena and Mar&iacute;a Lerena, named in 1908: <a href={u("/enrolment-card/")}>the enrolment card</a>, <a href={u("/people/juan-carlos-lerena/")}>Juan Carlos</a>, <a href={u("/people/maria-lerena/")}>Mar&iacute;a</a>.</li>
      <li>The Larena of Cayasta and Gualeguaych&uacute;: <a href={u("/name/#s-variants")}>every way the name has been written</a>.</li>
      <li>Carlos Lerena of Mendoza: <a href={u("/argentina/#s-mendoza-carlos")}>the Mendoza section</a>, <a href={u("/people/carlos-lerena/")}>Carlos</a>.</li>
      <li>The Lerena of Trinidad with no father named: <a href={u("/people/cayetana-faustina-lerena/")}>Cayetana Faustina</a>, <a href={u("/people/rodolfo-bernardo-lerena/")}>Rodolfo Bernardo</a>, <a href={u("/people/ismael-lerena/")}>Ismael</a>.</li>
      <li>Benito Lerena and Mar&iacute;a Cristina, Artigas: <a href={u("/uruguay/#s-artigas")}>Artigas, the department nobody had opened</a>.</li>
      <li>Pedro Lerena and Juana Camacho, San Jos&eacute;: <a href={u("/people/pedro-lerena/")}>Pedro</a>, <a href={u("/people/juana-camacho/")}>Juana</a>.</li>
      <li>Leandro Lerena and Luc&iacute;a Llanos, Buenos Aires: <a href={u("/argentina/#s-leandro-llanos")}>the census that broke a null</a>.</li>
      <li>Lerina, Italy: <a href={u("/name/#s-italy")}>Italy, and the Lerina spelling</a>. Lerena of Arequipa: <a href={u("/searched/")}>the full-text sweep</a>. Lerrena, Colombia: <a href={u("/name/#s-variants")}>every way the name has been written</a>. The Lerena y Lerena of Navarrete: <a href={u("/name/#s-navarrete")}>Navarrete, Logro&ntilde;o</a>.</li>
    </ul></div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:18px">What this drawing is <em>not</em>: a claim that the
    {nLines} lanes are separate families. <strong>Two of them may yet prove to be one</strong> &mdash;
    whether the Trinidad kindred joins Sancho&rsquo;s house is an open question with no evidence
    either way, which is why no join is drawn between them. <strong>An undrawn line is a statement
    too.</strong> <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a></div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
<h2 id="s-variants">Every way the name has been written down</h2>
<p class="dek">Fourteen forms, each one taken from a document this archive has read. Twelve are the
  same name; one is a different family; one is not a name at all.</p>
<div class="prose">
  <p><strong>This is the most practical page in the archive, and it was the last one written.</strong>
    For a month every sweep here was run on <strong>LERENA</strong>. On 12 October 2026 the same
    search run on <strong>SERENA</strong> returned children for two couples this archive had worked
    for weeks and had no children for &mdash; and the only thing that had hidden them was a letter.</p>
  <p><strong>One act of 1879 writes one family four ways on one page</strong> &mdash; <em>Lere&ntilde;a</em>
    for the father, <em>Serena</em> for the grandfather, <em>Levena</em> for the godfather,
    <em>Serena</em> again for the godmother. <strong>One Buenos Aires probate of 1889 writes one man
    as both <em>Lerena</em> and <em>Llerena</em>, twice each, in a single paragraph.</strong> After
    that, searching this surname by its letters is not a method.</p>
</div>
<dl class="kv" style="margin-top:18px">
  {dVariants.filter((r) => r["the form"]).map((r) => (
    <div><dt set:html={emph(r["the form"])} /><dd>
      <span set:html={emph(r["where this archive has met it"])} /><br />
      <span class="cap" set:html={emph(r["what it is, and what it cost or gave"])} />
    </dd></div>
  ))}
</dl>
<div class="prose" style="margin-top:18px">
  <p class="cap"><strong>What this table is for.</strong> Not completeness &mdash; a name can be
    mangled in more ways than anyone can list. It is for the next person who runs a search here and
    gets nothing: <strong>the null may be a letter rather than a fact</strong>, and these are the
    letters that have done it before. <a href={u("/method/")}>The method &rarr;</a></p>
</div>

<h2 id="s-sweep">What happened when all fourteen were used at once</h2>
<p class="dek">The table above was published on the morning of 12 October. Within the hour it was turned on the
  question this archive exists to answer, and the answer held.</p>
<div class="prose">
  <p>Every parent-pair search ever run here &mdash; the method that found Sancho&rsquo;s children,
    and the one that produced the sentence <em>&ldquo;Juan Carlos Lerena exists in exactly one
    record on earth&rdquo;</em> &mdash; set the surname to <strong>LERENA</strong> and nothing else.
    Thirteen spellings were available. <strong>So the whole grid was run: each spelling as the
    father&rsquo;s surname against each spelling as the mother&rsquo;s, 121 cells, both exact, no
    place filter and no date filter.</strong></p>
  <p><strong>One hundred and ten of those cells had never been asked.</strong> Twenty-four came
    back with something in them. Ten are a spelling against itself &mdash; other families&rsquo;
    pools, 1,346 Serenas and 299 Lorenas and 244 Llerenas. <strong>Fourteen are cells where the
    father is written one way and the mother another, and those fourteen held sixteen records.
    All sixteen were opened.</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Twelve are elsewhere and elsewhen</strong> &mdash; Cartagena, Magangu&eacute;,
    Houghton in Michigan, Freestone in Texas, Barcelona, Los Santos de Maimona, Torino, Cabugao in
    the Philippines, Cear&aacute;, Cogollos in Granada, Cusco. <strong>Two are Uruguayan, and both
    turned out to be real households of this name that nobody here had met.</strong></p>
</div>
<dl class="kv" style="margin-top:18px">
  {dSweep.filter((r) => r["what"]).map((r) => (
    <div><dt set:html={emph(r["what"])} /><dd>
      <span class="cap" set:html={emph(r["the query"])} /><br />
      <strong set:html={emph(r["result"])} /><br />
      <span set:html={emph(r["what the index said"])} /><br />
      <span set:html={emph(r["what the act says, or what it means"])} />
    </dd></div>
  ))}
</dl>
<div class="prose" style="margin-top:18px">
  <p class="cap"><strong>And the point of it.</strong> The sweep was run to find out whether the
    central null is a fact about the family or a fact about one spelling. <strong>It is a fact about
    the family</strong> &mdash; no Juan Carlos and no Mar&iacute;a in any of the thirteen, in either
    of the two indexes. That is worth more than the null it replaces, because it has now been asked
    in every way this archive knows how to ask it.
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>The open questions &rarr;</a></p>
</div>

<h2 id="s-how-rare">How rare</h2>
  <p>
    Forebears, accessed 8 September 2026: <strong>2,124 bearers worldwide</strong>, the
    <strong>197,177th</strong> commonest surname on earth, an incidence of <strong>1 in 3,431,048</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Set that beside the other families in this group of archives. <em>Falco</em> has
    <strong>46,749</strong> bearers and two independent origins, so no two Falco lines can be assumed
    related. <em>Lerena</em> is <strong>twenty-two times scarcer</strong>. The methodological consequence
    runs the other way:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    With a surname this rare, a cluster of Lerenas in one town is far more likely to be one family than to
    be coincidence. Connections may be <em>proposed</em> on distribution alone — but they must still be
    <em>proved</em> in the registers.
  </blockquote>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="contents">What is on this page</h2>
  <p class="dek">Where the surname is, where it is not, and what that is worth as evidence. The build checks this list against the page, so a section cannot be added or
    renamed without appearing here.</p>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:14px">
    <ol class="cols2">
      <li><a href="#s-lines">{NLines} lines of one name, drawn side by side</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s-variants">Every way the name has been written down</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s-sweep">What happened when all fourteen were used at once</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s-how-rare">How rare</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s-where-they-are">Where they are</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s-spain-one-cluster-and-it-is-not-where-you-would-guess">Spain: one cluster, and it is not where you would guess</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s-navarrete">Navarrete, Logro&ntilde;o &mdash; the one place the name was doubled</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s-what-the-name-means-the-unresolved-fork">What the name means — the unresolved fork</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s-who-the-lerenas-married-at-trinidad">Who the Lerenas married at Trinidad</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s-italy">Italy, and the Lerina spelling</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s-sources-for-this-page">Sources for this page</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s-two-spellings-one-town">Two spellings, one town</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s-the-forename-is-the-sharper-clue">The forename is the sharper clue</a></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>


<section class="blk">
<h2 id="s-where-they-are">Where they are</h2>
  <Distribution rows={dist}
    label="Where the Lerena surname is densest: bearers per head of population, by country"
  />
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:6px">Bar length is density. Beside each bar: bearers, incidence, and the name&rsquo;s rank in that country.</p>
  <p class="cap">
    Smaller presences are also recorded in Cuba, Brazil, Peru, Ecuador, Venezuela, England, Ireland,
    Canada, Switzerland, Belgium and the Philippines. Source: Forebears.
  </p>

  <h3>Read it by density, not by total</h3>
  <p>
    Spain has the largest absolute number, and that is what one would expect of the country of origin.
    But <strong>Uruguay's density is the highest in the world</strong> — 1 in 7,962, against Spain's
    1 in 52,649. A small population carrying a name at six times the homeland's density is the classic
    signature of <strong>founder emigration</strong>: a few people arrived, and their descendants are now a
    measurable fraction of the country.
  </p>
  <p>
    Uruguay and Argentina together make the <strong>River Plate</strong> the true modern heartland of the
    name. The family's own account — Rosario, on the Paraná — sits squarely inside it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>South Africa's 61</strong> deserve the same reading. For a surname of 2,124 people worldwide,
    61 in one country far from Iberia and the Americas is not background scatter. It looks like one
    family that arrived and multiplied. <a href={u("/south-africa/")}>South Africa &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2 id="s-spain-one-cluster-and-it-is-not-where-you-would-guess">Spain: one cluster, and it is not where you would guess</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> Forebears gives the Spanish distribution as
    <strong>La Rioja 27%</strong>, <strong>Andalusia 25%</strong> and <strong>Madrid 19%</strong> of the
    Spanish total. Read as raw percentages those look like three comparable strongholds. They are nothing
    of the sort. Andalusia and Madrid are simply <em>enormous</em>; La Rioja is tiny.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Region</th><th>Share</th><th>Bearers</th><th>Population</th><th>Incidence</th><th>vs. national</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>La Rioja</strong></td><td class="mono">27%</td><td class="mono">~240</td>
          <td class="mono">319,000</td><td class="mono"><strong>1:1,331</strong></td>
          <td class="mono"><strong>39.6&times;</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Andalusia</td><td class="mono">25%</td><td class="mono">~222</td>
          <td class="mono">8,402,000</td><td class="mono">1:37,848</td><td class="mono">1.4&times;</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Madrid</td><td class="mono">19%</td><td class="mono">~169</td>
          <td class="mono">6,454,000</td><td class="mono">1:38,255</td><td class="mono">1.4&times;</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="cap">Spain overall</td><td class="mono small">100%</td><td class="mono small">888</td>
          <td class="mono small">46,771,000</td><td class="mono small">1:52,670</td><td class="mono small">1.0&times;</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <strong>La Rioja carries the name at forty times the Spanish average.</strong> Andalusia and Madrid
    carry it at 1.4 times — which is to say, at about the rate you would expect if Lerenas were sprinkled
    evenly across Spain and those two regions simply held a lot of people. They are not strongholds. They
    are big.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    There is exactly one real Lerena cluster in Spain, and it is in <strong>La Rioja</strong>.
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    <strong>And on 11 October 2026 this archive finally put a household inside that cluster.</strong>
    The Spanish consulate at Buenos Aires recorded a power of attorney of 14 November 1900 by two
    brothers <strong>&ldquo;naturales de Navarrete, provincia de Logro&ntilde;o&rdquo;</strong> &mdash;
    and <strong>the province of Logro&ntilde;o is La Rioja</strong>, under the name it carried until
    1980. So the one place the statistics point at is the one place the documents landed, which is
    agreement rather than proof: it is where a Lerena in Spain is most likely to be found, so finding
    one there was always the likeliest outcome.
    <strong>What the household adds is not its region but its shape</strong> &mdash; mother, father
    and sons all surnamed Lerena. It is drawn with the lines above, under
    <a href="#s-lines">a different stock of the same name</a>, because nothing connects it to this
    family and every lane here is Banda Oriental stock documented from the 1790s.
  </p>
  <p class="cap">
    Densities calculated by this archive from Forebears' regional percentages and 2014 population figures;
    the arithmetic is in <span class="mono">data/spain-regional.tsv</span>. Forebears leaves the remaining
    29% of the Spanish total unallocated.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2 id="s-navarrete">Navarrete, Logro&ntilde;o &mdash; the one place the name was doubled</h2>
<p class="dek">A seam this archive has been pointing at for a day, worked on 12 October 2026.
  The registers turn out not to be filmed, and the one promising record turned out to be
  Guadalajara.</p>
<div class="prose">
  <p>At the Spanish consulate in Buenos Aires on 14 November 1900, the brothers
    <strong>Prudencio Benito</strong> and <strong>Juan Jos&eacute; LERENA y LERENA</strong>,
    <em>naturales de Navarrete, provincia de Logro&ntilde;o</em>, signed a power of attorney with
    their widowed mother <strong>Isabela LERENA y Basar&aacute;n</strong>. A Lerena had married a
    Lerena, in Spain, and the children carried it as <strong>a surname of record rather than as an
    indexer&rsquo;s slip</strong>. That makes Navarrete the one place this archive can name where
    the doubling was ordinary &mdash; which is why it was worth a day.</p>
  <p><strong>The answer is a hard one.</strong> FamilySearch&rsquo;s catalogue for Navarrete,
    Logro&ntilde;o holds <strong>one subject and one item</strong> &mdash; a census, the
    <em>Catastro de Ensenada</em> of 1752. <strong>No baptisms, no marriages, no burials.</strong>
    The full-text corpus has no Navarrete volume of any kind. So the registers cannot be read from
    this desk, and the seam now needs a letter rather than a session.</p>
  <p><strong>And the record that looked like a second instance was not one.</strong> The index
    offers a mother named <em>Wenceslaa Mar&iacute;a Lerena Lerena</em>, which would have made the
    usage regional rather than one household&rsquo;s. Read at the plate it is a marriage at
    <strong>San Gil de Guadalajara, 21 December 1863</strong> &mdash; two hundred kilometres away
    &mdash; and both the margin and the body write <strong>Lerena once</strong>. Her mother was a
    <em>Lozano</em>. <strong>The archive&rsquo;s own compound test survives it; this was the
    forty-fourth doubling, not the first Spanish compound.</strong></p>
</div>
<dl class="kv" style="margin-top:18px">
  {dNavarrete.filter((r) => r["what"]).map((r) => (
    <div><dt set:html={emph(r["what"])} /><dd>
      <span class="cap" set:html={emph(r["the query, or the source"])} /><br />
      <strong set:html={emph(r["result"])} /><br />
      <span set:html={emph(r["what was already known"])} /><br />
      <span set:html={emph(r["what it means, or what the act says"])} />
    </dd></div>
  ))}
</dl>
<div class="prose" style="margin-top:18px">
  <p class="cap"><strong>What is now on the list because of this.</strong> A letter to the
    <strong>Archivo Hist&oacute;rico Diocesano de Logro&ntilde;o</strong> for the Navarrete
    marriage and the three baptisms; a walk through the <strong>1752 Catastro</strong>
    (DGS 4386870 and 4386703), which cannot name a Juan Carlos but can say whether the name was
    at Navarrete at all before the nineteenth century; and an hour on a <strong>Spanish-born
    Lerena household living in Buenos Aires in 1895</strong>, five years before the brothers
    signed. <a href={u("/worklist/")}>The work list &rarr;</a></p>
</div>

<h2 id="s-what-the-name-means-the-unresolved-fork">What the name means — the unresolved fork</h2>
  <p>There are two candidate origins, and the archive does not yet know which applies here.</p>

  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">1. Basque: a house-name</h3>
      <p>
        The suffix <strong>-ena</strong> is the Basque possessive, "the house of" or "belonging to" — the
        formative behind <em>Perurena</em>, <em>Marinelarena</em>, <em>Arruabarrena</em> and hundreds more.
        On this reading <em>Lerena</em> is a <strong>house-name</strong>, and in Basque practice the house
        names the family, not the reverse.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        A Basque origin would also fit the destination. Basque emigration to the River Plate began in the
        1820s and ran for a century; it is estimated that up to a tenth of Uruguay's population has at
        least one Basque surname. <span class="chip inf">Plausible, unproven</span>
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">2. Castilian: a town in Extremadura</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Llerena</strong>, in Badajoz, is a real and significant town — seat of the Order of
        Santiago and of an Inquisitorial tribunal, with a large medieval Jewish and <em>converso</em>
        population. It has given its name to <strong>36,828</strong> bearers of <em>Llerena</em>.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        Forebears lists <em>Llerena</em> as the closest variant of <em>Lerena</em> at 92% similarity. The
        Castilian <em>Ll-</em> reduces to <em>L-</em> easily in Spanish-American registers and at ports of
        arrival, so a <em>Lerena</em> may simply be a <em>Llerena</em> written down by someone in a hurry.
        <span class="chip inf">Plausible, unproven</span>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>The Riojan cluster settles it, or very nearly</h3>
  <p>
    The two readings above were written before the Spanish regional figures were in hand. Those figures
    now discriminate between them, and they do not favour the town.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Against Llerena in Badajoz:</strong> if <em>Lerena</em> were a worn-down
      <em>Llerena</em>, the name should be thickest in and around <strong>Extremadura</strong>.
      <strong>Extremadura does not appear in the Spanish distribution at all.</strong> The 36,828
      <em>Llerena</em> bearers are a real and separate family of names; the evidence that ours reduced out of
      theirs is now weak.</li>
    <li><strong>For a Basque or upper-Ebro origin:</strong> the one genuine cluster is
      <strong>La Rioja</strong>, on the upper Ebro, immediately south of the Basque provinces and Navarre —
      exactly the country where the Basque possessive <strong>-ena</strong>, "the house of", is at home and
      where Basque house-names spill over into Castilian-speaking territory.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <span class="chip">Inferred</span> On the evidence now held, <em>Lerena</em> looks like a
    <strong>Basque-formed house-name of the upper Ebro</strong>, carried from La Rioja or its Basque
    borderlands to the River Plate, and not a reduction of the Extremaduran toponymic.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is <strong>inference from distribution, not proof from a register</strong>, and this archive has
    said throughout that rarity may propose but only documents dispose. It also does not yet touch
    <a href={u("/direct-line/")}>David's own line</a>, whose earliest certain member was born at
    <strong>Rosario in 1882</strong> and was Argentine by birth. What it does is tell the Spanish end of the
    search where to look first.
  </p>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--terra);margin:1.6rem 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">13 September 2026 &mdash; the second test was met, and it
      answered the other way</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>One of the questions below asks whether <em>Lerena</em> and <em>Llerena</em> ever alternate for
        one person in early River Plate records. They do. Following the 1866 Rosario baptism act back
        through the Montevideo family produced a trunk, and at its root a soldier:</p>
      <p style="font-size:1.05rem"><strong>Sancho Llerena Aguilar</strong>, born
        <strong>Córdoba, Andalusia, 1767</strong>, married at <strong>Canelones, Uruguay, 8 January
        1791</strong>, resident at Montevideo, died 1832.</p>
      <p>The Uruguayan parish registers spell him <strong>Lerena</strong> in 1793, 1840 and 1858;
        <strong>Llerena</strong> in 1804, 1811, 1827 and 1830; and once, in 1828,
        <strong><em>Sancho de Llerena</em></strong> &mdash; the toponymic form, <em>of Llerena</em>. A
        Basque house-name in <em>-ena</em> does not take <em>de</em>.</p>
      <p><strong>The distribution argument above still stands on its own terms.</strong> La Rioja really
        is the one dense Spanish cluster of the spelling <em>Lerena</em>, at forty times the national
        average, and that has to be something. What has changed is that it need not be
        <em>this</em> something. <strong>Both can be true:</strong> a Basque-formed <em>Lerena</em> of
        the upper Ebro, and separately a <strong>River Plate <em>Lerena</em> that is a reduction of
        Andalusian <em>Llerena</em></strong>, carried over by one man in the 1780s and smoothed to a
        single <em>l</em> by Uruguayan clerks inside a generation.</p>
      <p>For this archive, only the second bears on anything. The question was never where the name is
        dense in Spain. It was where <em>this family&rsquo;s</em> name came from &mdash; and the only
        Lerena trunk yet found in the River Plate is a <strong>Llerena</strong>.</p>
      <p class="cap"><strong>Tier and caveats, which matter here.</strong> The trunk comes from the
        FamilySearch Family Tree, which is user-submitted and editable by anyone, and the decision that
        those fourteen records are one man is a tree user&rsquo;s, not this archive&rsquo;s &mdash; the
        acts have not been opened. What anyone can check is the narrower fact: the Uruguayan registers
        spell this family both ways. And <strong>nothing connects that trunk to Pablo Armando</strong>;
        no Juan Carlos appears anywhere in it. <a href={u("/hypotheses/")}>Hypothesis 1 &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <h3>What would actually close it</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The <strong>Rosario baptism of 22 February 1882</strong>, which names Pablo Armando's parents —
      and, with luck, where they came from. <a href={u("/passport/")}>The passport &rarr;</a></li>
    <li><s>The family's <strong>earliest River Plate records</strong>: which spelling, and do
      <em>Lerena</em> and <em>Llerena</em> ever alternate for one person?</s>
      <strong>Answered 13 September &mdash; they alternate, for Sancho Llerena Aguilar, across forty
      years of Uruguayan registers.</strong> What remains is to open those acts rather than trust the
      attachments.</li>
    <li>An <strong>emigration record</strong> naming the town of origin outright.</li>
    <li>The <strong>La Rioja parish registers</strong> — now a specific place to look rather than a country.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2 id="s-who-the-lerenas-married-at-trinidad">Who the Lerenas married at Trinidad</h2>
<div class="prose">
  <p class="dek">13 September 2026. A small piece of evidence on the Basque-or-Extremaduran fork, from
    a direction the fork itself cannot reach.</p>
  <p>Everything above argues about where the <em>name</em> comes from. This is about the
    <strong>women the men of that name married</strong>, in the one town where the surname is densest
    on earth &mdash; <strong>Trinidad, in the department of Flores</strong>, Uruguay.</p>
  <p>The Trinidad baptisms were re-read by <em>birthplace</em> rather than by surname, which produced
    for the first time what the earlier source gave only as first names: <strong>the mothers&rsquo;
    surnames</strong>.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Father</th><th>Wife</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Bernab&eacute; Lerena</td><td><strong>Casimira PEL&Aacute;EZ</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Vitalino Lerena</td><td><strong>Andrea ECHEVERRY</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cipriano Lerena</td><td><strong>Lauriana BELOQUI</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ysaac Lerena</td><td>Adelaida Gazo</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ferm&iacute;n Lerena</td><td>Paulina Barrag&aacute;n</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Florencio Lerena</td><td>Juana Recuero</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><strong>Beloqui and Echeverry are Basque</strong> &mdash;
    <em>Beloki</em> and <em>Etxeberri</em> are among the most ordinary surnames in Gipuzkoa and Navarre.
    Pel&aacute;ez is not Basque, but it is the family the Lerenas married <em>repeatedly</em>: by 1885
    the two had to be <strong>dispensed for consanguinity</strong> to marry again, twice in the third
    degree and once in the second and third accumulated. They were one intermarried kindred.</div>
  <p style="margin-top:14px"><strong>What this is worth, stated carefully.</strong> It is evidence
    about the <em>community</em> these Lerenas married into, and that is a different thing from the
    etymology of their name. A family can be Extremaduran by name and marry Basque neighbours; the
    River Plate was full of both. So this <strong>does not settle the fork above</strong>, and it is not
    offered as settling it.</p>
  <p class="cap">What it does do is put a thumb on one side of a scale that has had nothing on it but
    toponymy. Until now the Basque case rested entirely on how the word looks. This is the first
    evidence of the family <em>keeping Basque company</em>.</p>
</div>

